# Intro
One 29.7 Msun star at the centre of an isolated box (no periodic images) of cold (50 K), zero-metallicity molecular
gas with 8 dense Gaussian clumps (peak density 100 times the clear gas, clumps 0.12 to 0.32 box
sizes from the star). The ISRF transport is on (`ISRF_propagation: 1`) with the H2 line absorption
of the transport on (`ISRF_H2_line_absorption: 1`), so the Lyman-Werner (LW) light of the star is
absorbed by the H2 lines along its path.

At a limited light speed (`ISRF_packet_light_speed: fixed` or `cap`) the light that has not yet
reached a gas particle waits in a reception slot of that particle. When part of the waiting light
is released, the released light receives the mean H2 shielding of the whole pool. This example
runs the cloud twice from the same initial conditions, with a limited and with an unlimited light
speed (no light waits), and checks the first against the second.

Read the README of `ISRFH2StarLit` for the radiation table and the exit classes. This run needs
geajay, as that example does.

# Configure
Use the build of `ISRFH2StarLit`.

# Run
```
./run.sh
light_speed=cap ./run.sh      # limited arm with the cap mode instead of 0.99 c
```
`run.sh` fetches the glass file and the tables, writes the initial conditions, runs the
unlimited arm (directory `control`), then the limited arm (directory `limited`), then the check.
Both arms write snapshots at the same times. `level`, `gas_density`, `gas_mass`, `h2_ratio`
(initial `nH2/nH`, default 3e-5) and `n_threads` override the defaults. The run keeps the gas
cold and the H2 nearly frozen: photoionization, winds and supernovae are off, the metallicity is
zero, and the time step is set by the light speed (64 steps of 1.47e-10 internal units).

# Checking
`python3 checkH2WaitingLight.py --limited limited --control control`

The box is not periodic in both runs (`InitialConditions:periodic: 0`). In a periodic box the
light of the periodic images of the star reaches a particle through neighbours that are farther from
the star than the particle. Those neighbours are not shares, so the control run would leave the range
of its own shares once the image light arrives (about 0.55 to 0.95 box sizes divided by the speed of
the front). The check stops with exit class 4 if either run is periodic.

The check reads every snapshot that both runs hold, in time order, from the first one at which
at least 200 particles are lit in both runs. Particles are read between 2 smoothing lengths and
half a box minus 2 smoothing lengths from the star, and only if their H2 density has changed by
at most 5 per cent since the first snapshot.

The window of snapshots ends at the first snapshot where either of two things happens, and the
check prints the window and the reason: the control run has a lit particle outside the range of its
own shares (the quantity the comparison relies on), or the H2 of more than half the compared particles
has changed by more than 5 per cent. A snapshot at the end of the window and later snapshots are not
read. If neither happens, the window ends at the last snapshot that both runs hold.

A particle receives light as shares from the neighbours that are closer to the star. A share has
the H2 column carried by its sender plus the column of the hop. The released light is a weighted
mean of the shares of the pool, so the transmission and the column of a particle lie between the
smallest and the largest share value. The shares are built from the carried columns of the
senders of the same run in all snapshots up to the compared one, because a share may
have been sent, and left waiting, a few steps earlier.

- `hull_t`: `|ln(t_limited / t_control)|` is at most `ln(largest / smallest)` of the share
  transmissions of both runs.
- `hull_N`: the carried column of both runs lies between the smallest and the largest share column.
- `own range`: the limited run alone lies inside the range of its own shares (transmission and column).
- The control run must obey the range of its own shares, tested on all its lit particles in the
  read region.
- The two snapshots compared must have the same time. The limited run must have lit fewer
  particles than the control run at the first compared snapshot, else no light waits and the run is void.

The shares are built from carried columns of the same run, so light that is older than the
snapshot (waiting, or delayed by the front) is read with the column it had when it was sent. The
check does not compare a column with the straight line from the star. It does not test whether the
front of the `cap` mode is slower than 0.99 c (light older than light at 0.99 c): both runs use their
own shares.

The exit class is 0 for a pass, 1 for a failed bar, 2 for a missing file or field, 3 for a
non-finite value and 4 for a void run (a premise or the positive control is not met).
